<BaseLayout title="About — Skyhook Industries" description="About Skyhook Industries, an independent engineering project investigating reusable orbital transportation with rotating momentum-exchange tethers.">
  <div class="shell">
    <section class="page-hero"><p class="eyebrow">About</p><h1>Revisiting an unusually powerful idea.</h1><p>Skyhook Industries is an independent effort to examine whether decades of space-tether research can be translated into a modern, testable architecture for reusable orbital transportation.</p></section>
    <section class="page-section prose"><h2>Why Skyhook?</h2><p>Launch vehicles have improved enormously, but nearly every mission still carries the machinery and reaction mass needed for each major velocity change. A momentum-exchange tether asks a different question: what transportation work becomes possible if part of that machinery is built once and left in orbit for repeated use?</p><p>The concept is ambitious enough that credibility matters. We do not describe analytical studies as flight demonstrations, design targets as specifications, or unresolved materials problems as solved engineering.</p></section>
    <section class="page-section prose"><p class="eyebrow">Founder</p><h2>Isaac A. Mattoo</h2><p>Skyhook Industries was founded by Isaac A. Mattoo. His professional background is in complex infrastructure and systems engineering, with a focus on automation, reliability and fault-tolerant systems.</p><p>The project applies the same instinct to orbital infrastructure: expose assumptions, design around failure, test progressively, and prefer systems that can be inspected and improved over one-shot heroics.</p></section>
    <section class="page-section prose"><p class="eyebrow">Engineering principles</p><h2>How we intend to work.</h2><ul class="list-clean"><li><b>Test how you fly</b><span>Increase environmental and operational fidelity as a design matures.</span></li><li><b>Design for failure</b><span>Use redundant load paths, graceful degradation and replaceable subsystems where practical.</span></li><li><b>Shorten the path to evidence</b><span>Break large problems into tests that retire specific risks.</span></li><li><b>Optimize for learning</b><span>A prototype does not need to be perfect. It needs to answer a question.</span></li></ul></section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
